{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently above realized prices across the whole 30d horizon (typically +1 to +4 EUR, widening in selloffs). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when history shows a sharp decline, the model projects stabilization/rebound too early; realized continues falling. Increase negative momentum persistence (carry recent slope forward) and delay mean-reversion.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model often predicts stronger mid-horizon rebounds (days ~15–30) than occur; realized either rebounds less or keeps drifting lower. Damp rebound amplitude, especially after drawdowns.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon—near-term is closer, but medium/long horizon becomes increasingly optimistic. Use a bias term that becomes more negative with lead time (e.g., linear with horizon).",
    "Volatility miscalibration after shocks: after large moves, model path is too smooth and too high; realized shows deeper continuation. Increase downside tail/continuation risk following high-volatility or gap-down periods."
  ]
}
